{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-89",
              "name": "SQL Injection in Login Endpoint Enables Authentication Bypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL Injection in Login Endpoint Enables Authentication Bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A critical SQL injection vulnerability exists in the authentication endpoint at POST /rest/user/login. The login handler constructs a raw SQL query by directly interpolating the user-supplied email address into the query string without parameterization or input sanitization. This was identified as vuln-0001 in prior static analysis and has been confirmed exploitable against the live instance.\n\nThe vulnerable code on line 34 of routes/login.ts builds a SELECT query against the Users table by embedding `req.body.email` directly into the SQL template literal:\n```\nSELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = '${req.body.email || ''}' AND password = '${security.hash(req.body.password || '')}' AND deletedAt IS NULL\n```\n\nAn attacker can supply a crafted email value such as `' OR 1=1--` to manipulate the SQL query logic, causing the WHERE clause to always evaluate to true and return the first user record (the administrator account). This grants full administrative access without knowledge of any valid credentials."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A critical SQL injection vulnerability exists in the authentication endpoint at POST /rest/user/login. The login handler constructs a raw SQL query by directly interpolating the user-supplied email address into the query string without parameterization or input sanitization. This was identified as vuln-0001 in prior static analysis and has been confirmed exploitable against the live instance.\n\nThe vulnerable code on line 34 of routes/login.ts builds a SELECT query against the Users table by embedding `req.body.email` directly into the SQL template literal:\n```\nSELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = '${req.body.email || ''}' AND password = '${security.hash(req.body.password || '')}' AND deletedAt IS NULL\n```\n\nAn attacker can supply a crafted email value such as `' OR 1=1--` to manipulate the SQL query logic, causing the WHERE clause to always evaluate to true and return the first user record (the administrator account). This grants full administrative access without knowledge of any valid credentials.\n\nSuccessful exploitation enables complete authentication bypass. An attacker can:\n\n- Gain immediate administrative access to the application without any valid credentials\n- Access any user account by targeting specific email addresses with comment-based password bypass (e.g., `victim@email.com'--`)\n- Enumerate all user accounts through error-based or time-based blind injection techniques\n- Potentially extract sensitive data from the underlying SQLite database through more advanced injection payloads\n\nThe business impact is severe: full compromise of the authentication mechanism, access to all customer data, ability to perform administrative actions, and complete loss of confidentiality and integrity for all user accounts.\n\n1. Replace the raw SQL query with a parameterized query using Sequelize's built-in parameter binding:\n\n```typescript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  'SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = :email AND password = :password AND deletedAt IS NULL',\n  {\n    replacements: { email: req.body.email || '', password: security.hash(req.body.password || '') },\n    model: UserModel,\n    plain: true\n  }\n)\n```\n\n2. Alternatively, use Sequelize's ORM methods which automatically parameterize inputs:\n\n```typescript\nUserModel.findOne({\n  where: {\n    email: req.body.email || '',\n    password: security.hash(req.body.password || ''),\n    deletedAt: null\n  }\n})\n```\n\n3. Implement input validation on the email field to reject values containing SQL metacharacters before they reach the query layer.\n\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege to the database connection to limit the impact of any residual injection vectors.\n\n5. Deploy a Web Application Firewall (WAF) as a defense-in-depth measure to detect and block common SQL injection patterns."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Hardcoded RSA Private Key Enables JWT Token Forgery and Full Account Takeover",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded RSA Private Key Enables JWT Token Forgery and Full Account Takeover"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A critical cryptographic key management failure was identified in the application's authentication system. The RSA private key used for signing JWT tokens is hardcoded as a string literal in the application source code (lib/insecurity.ts, line 23). Since the Juice Shop repository is publicly available, any attacker with access to the source code can extract this key and forge arbitrary JWT tokens, impersonating any user—including administrators—without requiring valid credentials.\n\nThis finding confirms prior SAST finding vuln-0013. Dynamic validation demonstrated that tokens signed with the extracted private key are accepted by all authenticated endpoints in the running application."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A critical cryptographic key management failure was identified in the application's authentication system. The RSA private key used for signing JWT tokens is hardcoded as a string literal in the application source code (lib/insecurity.ts, line 23). Since the Juice Shop repository is publicly available, any attacker with access to the source code can extract this key and forge arbitrary JWT tokens, impersonating any user—including administrators—without requiring valid credentials.\n\nThis finding confirms prior SAST finding vuln-0013. Dynamic validation demonstrated that tokens signed with the extracted private key are accepted by all authenticated endpoints in the running application.\n\nAn attacker who obtains the hardcoded private key can:\n\n- Forge JWT tokens for any user account, including administrators, achieving full account takeover without credentials\n- Gain unrestricted access to all authenticated endpoints and administrative functionality\n- Persist access indefinitely by generating new tokens at will, regardless of password changes or session invalidation\n- Access sensitive user data (email addresses, password hashes, security answers) through administrative endpoints\n- Modify application state (products, user profiles, orders) through privileged API calls\n\nThe business impact is severe: complete compromise of all user accounts, administrative control of the application, and exposure of all user data stored within the system.\n\n1. Remove hardcoded cryptographic keys from source code immediately. Generate new RSA key pairs using a cryptographically secure random number generator with adequate key length (minimum 2048 bits).\n\n2. Store private keys in a secrets management system (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault) or in environment variables that are never committed to version control.\n\n3. Implement key rotation procedures. Rotate signing keys periodically and support multiple active verification keys during transition periods.\n\n4. Add the existing keys to a revocation list and force re-authentication for all active sessions after key rotation.\n\n5. Consider implementing additional token binding (e.g., audience claims, issuer validation, fingerprinting) to limit the impact of key compromise.\n\n6. Remove the hardcoded HMAC secret ('pa4qacea4VK9t9nGv7yZtwmj') used for security answer hashing and replace it with a properly managed secret."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-915",
              "name": "Mass Assignment Privilege Escalation via User Registration Endpoint",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Mass Assignment Privilege Escalation via User Registration Endpoint"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A mass assignment vulnerability exists in the user registration endpoint that permits unauthenticated attackers to create accounts with arbitrary privilege levels, including full administrative access.\n\nThis is a newly discovered runtime finding not covered by prior static analysis. The POST /api/Users/ endpoint accepts and persists the \"role\" field directly from user-supplied JSON input during account creation. No server-side filtering or allowlisting of writable fields is applied before the Sequelize ORM creates the user record, enabling an unauthenticated attacker to set role=admin and gain full administrative privileges."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A mass assignment vulnerability exists in the user registration endpoint that permits unauthenticated attackers to create accounts with arbitrary privilege levels, including full administrative access.\n\nThis is a newly discovered runtime finding not covered by prior static analysis. The POST /api/Users/ endpoint accepts and persists the \"role\" field directly from user-supplied JSON input during account creation. No server-side filtering or allowlisting of writable fields is applied before the Sequelize ORM creates the user record, enabling an unauthenticated attacker to set role=admin and gain full administrative privileges.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can create accounts with administrative privileges by including the \"role\" field set to \"admin\" in the registration request. This grants:\n\n- Full administrative access to the application\n- Ability to view and manage all user accounts\n- Ability to modify product catalog, pricing, and descriptions\n- Access to administrative configuration and internal endpoints\n- Complete bypass of the intended role-based access control model\n\nThe vulnerability requires no authentication, making it trivially exploitable by any external attacker.\n\n1. Implement an explicit field allowlist for user registration that excludes sensitive fields:\n   - Only permit `email`, `password`, `passwordRepeat`, and `securityQuestion`/`securityAnswer` during self-service registration\n   - Strip or reject any request body fields not in the allowlist (especially `role`, `isActive`, `deluxeToken`)\n\n2. Add server-side enforcement in the registration middleware:\n   - Before passing `req.body` to the model, delete any properties that should not be user-controllable\n   - Example: `delete req.body.role; delete req.body.isActive;`\n\n3. Configure the Sequelize model or ORM layer to distinguish mass-assignable fields from protected fields:\n   - Use a DTO pattern or explicit `fields` option in `Model.create()` to restrict which attributes are set from external input\n\n4. Apply defense-in-depth by checking the caller's role before allowing elevated role assignment on any endpoint that can modify the `role` field."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-915"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/915.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-620",
              "name": "Authentication Bypass via Missing Current Password Validation in Password Change",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Authentication Bypass via Missing Current Password Validation in Password Change"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The password change endpoint at `GET /rest/user/change-password` permits changing a user's password without verifying the current password. The conditional check on line 39 of the `changePassword` handler uses a short-circuit evaluation pattern: `if (currentPassword && ...)`. When the `current` query parameter is omitted or left empty, the expression evaluates to falsy, and the entire verification block is skipped.\n\nThis effectively allows any authenticated session to reset the associated user's password to an attacker-controlled value without knowledge of the existing credential. Combined with cross-site request forgery (the endpoint uses GET method with query parameters) or session hijacking, this enables full account takeover.\n\nThis finding confirms prior SAST finding vuln-0002."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The password change endpoint at `GET /rest/user/change-password` permits changing a user's password without verifying the current password. The conditional check on line 39 of the `changePassword` handler uses a short-circuit evaluation pattern: `if (currentPassword && ...)`. When the `current` query parameter is omitted or left empty, the expression evaluates to falsy, and the entire verification block is skipped.\n\nThis effectively allows any authenticated session to reset the associated user's password to an attacker-controlled value without knowledge of the existing credential. Combined with cross-site request forgery (the endpoint uses GET method with query parameters) or session hijacking, this enables full account takeover.\n\nThis finding confirms prior SAST finding vuln-0002.\n\nAn attacker who obtains any valid session token (e.g., via XSS, session fixation, or network interception) can permanently take over the associated account by changing its password without knowing the current one. Additionally, since the endpoint uses GET with query parameters, it is trivially exploitable via cross-site request forgery—a malicious link or image tag in an email or web page could change a logged-in user's password without any interaction beyond visiting the page.\n\nBusiness impact includes:\n- Complete account takeover for any authenticated user\n- CSRF-based mass password reset if combined with session persistence\n- Loss of user trust and potential regulatory compliance violations\n\n1. Require the current password parameter unconditionally. Reject the request with 401 if the `current` parameter is missing or empty, regardless of other conditions.\n\n2. Change the HTTP method from GET to POST or PUT. Password change operations are state-changing and must not use GET, which is susceptible to CSRF via link/image tags and is logged in browser history and server access logs.\n\n3. Implement anti-CSRF protections (e.g., synchronizer token pattern or SameSite cookie attribute) on the password change endpoint.\n\n4. Apply rate limiting to prevent brute-force attempts against the current password verification.\n\n5. Consider requiring re-authentication (fresh login) before allowing password changes as an additional defense-in-depth measure."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-620"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/620.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-611",
              "name": "XML External Entity (XXE) Injection via File Upload Endpoint",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "XML External Entity (XXE) Injection via File Upload Endpoint"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An XML External Entity (XXE) injection vulnerability was confirmed in the file upload endpoint at POST /file-upload. The application accepts XML file uploads and parses them using the libxmljs2 library with external entity resolution explicitly enabled via the `noent: true` parser option.\n\nWhen a user uploads a file with a `.xml` extension, the server-side XML parser resolves external entities defined in the document's DOCTYPE declaration. The resolved content is then reflected in the HTTP error response body, creating a direct channel for data exfiltration.\n\nThis vulnerability was identified as vuln-0009 in prior static analysis and has been dynamically confirmed against the running instance. The vulnerability is gated only by the `deprecatedInterfaceChallenge` feature flag, which is enabled in all environments (no disabledEnv restriction)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An XML External Entity (XXE) injection vulnerability was confirmed in the file upload endpoint at POST /file-upload. The application accepts XML file uploads and parses them using the libxmljs2 library with external entity resolution explicitly enabled via the `noent: true` parser option.\n\nWhen a user uploads a file with a `.xml` extension, the server-side XML parser resolves external entities defined in the document's DOCTYPE declaration. The resolved content is then reflected in the HTTP error response body, creating a direct channel for data exfiltration.\n\nThis vulnerability was identified as vuln-0009 in prior static analysis and has been dynamically confirmed against the running instance. The vulnerability is gated only by the `deprecatedInterfaceChallenge` feature flag, which is enabled in all environments (no disabledEnv restriction).\n\nSuccessful exploitation enables an attacker to:\n\n- Read arbitrary files from the server filesystem, including /etc/passwd, application configuration files, and cryptographic key material (the JWT public key was confirmed readable).\n- Perform server-side request forgery (SSRF) against internal network services by substituting file:// URIs with http:// or other protocol schemes.\n- Cause denial-of-service through recursive entity expansion (billion laughs attack), which the application partially mitigates with a 2-second execution timeout.\n- Exfiltrate sensitive application data including database credentials, API keys, and environment variables accessible to the application process.\n\nThe attack requires only valid authentication (any registered user account) and directly compromises server-side confidentiality.\n\n1. Disable external entity resolution in the XML parser by removing the `noent: true` option from the libxmljs2 parseXml call. The secure configuration should be:\n   ```javascript\n   libxml.parseXml(data, { noblanks: true, noent: false, nocdata: true, nonet: true })\n   ```\n\n2. Add the `nonet: true` option to prevent the XML parser from making any network connections, blocking SSRF via XXE.\n\n3. Implement strict file type validation that rejects XML uploads entirely if XML processing is not a business requirement for the complaint submission workflow.\n\n4. If XML processing is required, use a defused XML parser or configure the parser to disable all external entity resolution, DTD processing, and XInclude support.\n\n5. Remove the reflection of parsed XML content in error responses to eliminate the direct exfiltration channel even if entity resolution cannot be fully disabled."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-916",
              "name": "Insecure Password Storage Using Unsalted MD5 Hashing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure Password Storage Using Unsalted MD5 Hashing"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The application stores all user passwords using the MD5 hashing algorithm without any salt. The `hash` function in `lib/insecurity.ts` (line 43) applies a single round of MD5 to plaintext passwords before database storage. MD5 is a cryptographically broken hash function with known collision attacks, and the absence of a per-user salt means identical passwords produce identical hashes across all accounts.\n\nDynamic validation confirmed that the MD5 hash of known passwords (admin123, ncc-1701, OhG0dPlease1nsertLiquor!, TestPassword123) exactly matches the stored hashes returned in the JWT token payload for the respective accounts. Furthermore, the password hashes are exposed directly within the JWT token claims, compounding the risk.\n\nThis finding confirms prior SAST finding vuln-0011."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The application stores all user passwords using the MD5 hashing algorithm without any salt. The `hash` function in `lib/insecurity.ts` (line 43) applies a single round of MD5 to plaintext passwords before database storage. MD5 is a cryptographically broken hash function with known collision attacks, and the absence of a per-user salt means identical passwords produce identical hashes across all accounts.\n\nDynamic validation confirmed that the MD5 hash of known passwords (admin123, ncc-1701, OhG0dPlease1nsertLiquor!, TestPassword123) exactly matches the stored hashes returned in the JWT token payload for the respective accounts. Furthermore, the password hashes are exposed directly within the JWT token claims, compounding the risk.\n\nThis finding confirms prior SAST finding vuln-0011.\n\nThe use of unsalted MD5 for password storage exposes the entire user base to efficient offline credential compromise:\n\n- Rainbow table attacks can reverse any MD5 hash in seconds using precomputed tables\n- Identical passwords across users produce identical hashes, enabling batch cracking\n- MD5 computation is extremely fast on modern hardware (billions of hashes per second on GPUs), making brute-force trivial\n- Password hashes are additionally leaked in JWT token payloads, meaning any intercepted token reveals the password hash\n\nIn a breach scenario, an attacker with database access (or token interception capability) could recover plaintext passwords for the majority of users within minutes, enabling mass account compromise across this and other services where users reuse passwords.\n\n1. Replace MD5 with a modern password hashing algorithm designed for credential storage, such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2id. These algorithms incorporate per-hash salts and configurable work factors that make brute-force computationally expensive.\n\n2. Implement automatic migration of existing password hashes. On successful login with the old MD5 hash, re-hash the password with the new algorithm and update the stored value.\n\n3. Remove password hashes from JWT token payloads. Authentication tokens should never contain sensitive credential material. Include only the minimum claims necessary for authorization (user ID, role, expiration).\n\n4. Configure an appropriate work factor for the chosen algorithm (e.g., bcrypt cost factor of 12 or higher) that balances security with acceptable login latency.\n\n5. Implement monitoring for credential stuffing attacks, and consider offering users the option to check their passwords against known breach databases (e.g., Have I Been Pwned)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-916"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/916.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-918",
              "name": "Server-Side Request Forgery via Profile Image URL Upload",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Server-Side Request Forgery via Profile Image URL Upload"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A server-side request forgery (SSRF) vulnerability was confirmed in the profile image URL upload functionality at POST /profile/image/url. This validates prior SAST finding vuln-0005.\n\nThe application accepts a user-supplied URL via the `imageUrl` parameter and performs an unrestricted server-side HTTP fetch using Node.js native `fetch()`. No validation is applied to the destination address, port, protocol, or response content. The server-side nature of the request was conclusively confirmed by observing inbound connections from the application server (IP 172.17.0.6, User-Agent: \"node\") to a controlled listener.\n\nWhen the fetch succeeds, the response body is written directly to the filesystem as the user's profile image file, regardless of the actual content type. This file is then publicly accessible via the web server, creating a complete data exfiltration channel through which an attacker can retrieve the response body of any internal service request."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A server-side request forgery (SSRF) vulnerability was confirmed in the profile image URL upload functionality at POST /profile/image/url. This validates prior SAST finding vuln-0005.\n\nThe application accepts a user-supplied URL via the `imageUrl` parameter and performs an unrestricted server-side HTTP fetch using Node.js native `fetch()`. No validation is applied to the destination address, port, protocol, or response content. The server-side nature of the request was conclusively confirmed by observing inbound connections from the application server (IP 172.17.0.6, User-Agent: \"node\") to a controlled listener.\n\nWhen the fetch succeeds, the response body is written directly to the filesystem as the user's profile image file, regardless of the actual content type. This file is then publicly accessible via the web server, creating a complete data exfiltration channel through which an attacker can retrieve the response body of any internal service request.\n\nSuccessful exploitation enables an authenticated attacker to:\n\n- Make arbitrary HTTP requests from the application server to any network-reachable host (internal services, other microservices, databases with HTTP interfaces, admin panels)\n- Exfiltrate response data from internal services by retrieving the saved profile image file, which contains the raw response body\n- Perform internal network reconnaissance and port scanning by observing response timing and error behavior\n- Access services bound exclusively to the loopback interface (127.0.0.1) or internal network ranges\n- Potentially pivot to additional services in the same network segment\n\nIn cloud-hosted deployments, this class of vulnerability can lead to credential theft via instance metadata services, though such targets were out of scope for this assessment.\n\n1. Implement a strict URL allowlist that permits only approved external domains for profile image fetching. Reject all other destinations by default.\n\n2. After DNS resolution, validate the resolved IP address against blocked ranges before initiating the connection. Block all private (RFC 1918), loopback (127.0.0.0/8), and link-local (169.254.0.0/16) address ranges for both IPv4 and IPv6.\n\n3. Re-validate the destination address on every HTTP redirect hop. Do not follow redirects to disallowed destinations.\n\n4. Enforce response content-type validation. Only accept responses with recognized image MIME types (image/jpeg, image/png, image/gif, image/svg+xml) and reject all other content types before writing to disk.\n\n5. Apply response size limits to prevent abuse as a proxy for large data transfers.\n\n6. Consider using a dedicated egress proxy for outbound image fetches that enforces network policy and provides audit logging."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Broken Function-Level Authorization Allows Non-Admin Product Modification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broken Function-Level Authorization Allows Non-Admin Product Modification"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A broken function-level authorization vulnerability exists in the product management API that permits any authenticated user to modify product records regardless of their role. The PUT /api/Products/:id endpoint lacks authorization middleware, allowing non-administrative users to alter product names, descriptions, pricing, and images.\n\nThis is a newly discovered runtime finding not covered by prior static analysis. The authorization middleware for the PUT route on products is commented out in the server configuration, leaving the endpoint entirely unprotected beyond basic authentication. Additionally, the GET /api/Users/ endpoint only enforces authentication (not admin role), exposing all user account details to any authenticated caller."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A broken function-level authorization vulnerability exists in the product management API that permits any authenticated user to modify product records regardless of their role. The PUT /api/Products/:id endpoint lacks authorization middleware, allowing non-administrative users to alter product names, descriptions, pricing, and images.\n\nThis is a newly discovered runtime finding not covered by prior static analysis. The authorization middleware for the PUT route on products is commented out in the server configuration, leaving the endpoint entirely unprotected beyond basic authentication. Additionally, the GET /api/Users/ endpoint only enforces authentication (not admin role), exposing all user account details to any authenticated caller.\n\nAny authenticated user, regardless of assigned role, can:\n\n- Modify any product's name, description, price, and image in the catalog\n- Manipulate pricing to purchase items at reduced cost or deface product listings\n- Access the complete user directory (GET /api/Users/) exposing emails, roles, profile images, and account status of all registered users\n- Undermine data integrity of the product catalog and erode trust in the platform\n\nThis constitutes a horizontal and vertical privilege escalation since product modification is an administrative function that should be restricted to users with the admin role.\n\n1. Uncomment and strengthen the authorization middleware for PUT /api/Products/:id to require admin role:\n   - Replace the commented-out `security.isAuthorized()` with a role-checking middleware such as `security.isAdmin()`\n   - Ensure the middleware validates both token authenticity and admin role membership\n\n2. Add role-based authorization to the GET /api/Users/ endpoint:\n   - Replace `security.isAuthorized()` with an admin-only check\n   - Non-admin users should not be able to enumerate the user directory\n\n3. Review all API routes registered via the REST framework to ensure each has appropriate role-based authorization:\n   - Audit every route where `security.isAuthorized()` is used alone — this only checks authentication, not authorization\n   - Apply principle of least privilege: default to deny, then explicitly allow per role\n\n4. Implement centralized authorization middleware that enforces role checks consistently across all administrative endpoints."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-158",
              "name": "Null Byte Injection Bypasses File Type Restriction on FTP File Server",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Null Byte Injection Bypasses File Type Restriction on FTP File Server"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A null byte injection vulnerability exists in the FTP file server endpoint that allows an attacker to bypass the file type allowlist and download arbitrary files from the /ftp directory. The application restricts file downloads to .md and .pdf extensions, but an encoded null byte (%2500) injected before a permitted extension circumvents this restriction, enabling access to sensitive backup files, configuration data, and application source artifacts.\n\nThis is a newly discovered runtime finding not covered by prior static analysis. The vulnerability stems from an order-of-operations flaw: the extension allowlist check is performed on the raw (null-byte-containing) filename, while the actual file served has the null byte (and everything after it) stripped."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A null byte injection vulnerability exists in the FTP file server endpoint that allows an attacker to bypass the file type allowlist and download arbitrary files from the /ftp directory. The application restricts file downloads to .md and .pdf extensions, but an encoded null byte (%2500) injected before a permitted extension circumvents this restriction, enabling access to sensitive backup files, configuration data, and application source artifacts.\n\nThis is a newly discovered runtime finding not covered by prior static analysis. The vulnerability stems from an order-of-operations flaw: the extension allowlist check is performed on the raw (null-byte-containing) filename, while the actual file served has the null byte (and everything after it) stripped.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can download any file stored in the /ftp directory regardless of its extension, including:\n\n- package.json.bak — Application backup containing dependency information and internal metadata\n- coupons_2013.md.bak — Historical coupon codes that may still be valid\n- suspicious_errors.yml — Error logs potentially containing stack traces and internal paths\n- eastere.gg — Hidden application content\n- encrypt.pyc — Compiled Python bytecode potentially containing encryption logic\n\nExposure of backup files and configuration artifacts can reveal internal application structure, dependency versions (enabling targeted CVE exploitation), and business-sensitive data such as coupon codes or encryption routines.\n\n1. Reverse the order of operations: sanitize the filename (strip null bytes and normalize encoding) before performing the allowlist check:\n   - Call `cutOffPoisonNullByte()` first, then check the sanitized result against the extension allowlist\n   - This ensures the allowlist is evaluated against the actual filename that will be served\n\n2. Apply strict input validation on the filename parameter:\n   - Reject filenames containing percent-encoded sequences that decode to control characters\n   - Use a positive character allowlist (alphanumeric, hyphen, underscore, single dot for extension)\n\n3. Validate the final resolved file path against an explicit allowlist of permitted files rather than relying solely on extension checks.\n\n4. Remove sensitive files from the /ftp directory that should not be publicly accessible under any circumstances."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-158"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/158.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) in Shopping Basket Access",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) in Shopping Basket Access"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability exists in the shopping basket retrieval endpoint at `GET /rest/basket/:id`. The endpoint accepts a basket ID from the URL path parameter and returns the full basket contents — including product details, quantities, and pricing — without verifying that the requesting user is the owner of that basket.\n\nAny authenticated user can access any other user's basket by simply changing the numeric basket ID in the URL. The endpoint requires a valid JWT token in the Authorization header (returning HTTP 401 otherwise), but performs no ownership validation once authentication succeeds.\n\nThis finding confirms prior static-analysis finding vuln-0007."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability exists in the shopping basket retrieval endpoint at `GET /rest/basket/:id`. The endpoint accepts a basket ID from the URL path parameter and returns the full basket contents — including product details, quantities, and pricing — without verifying that the requesting user is the owner of that basket.\n\nAny authenticated user can access any other user's basket by simply changing the numeric basket ID in the URL. The endpoint requires a valid JWT token in the Authorization header (returning HTTP 401 otherwise), but performs no ownership validation once authentication succeeds.\n\nThis finding confirms prior static-analysis finding vuln-0007.\n\nAn authenticated attacker can enumerate and access all shopping baskets in the system by iterating over sequential basket IDs. This exposes:\n\n- Product selections and quantities for all users, revealing purchasing intent and preferences\n- Pricing information including any applied discounts or coupons\n- User IDs linked to baskets, enabling correlation of shopping behavior to specific accounts\n- Potential for further abuse including basket manipulation if PUT/POST endpoints share the same access control gap\n\nIn an e-commerce context, this represents a significant privacy violation and potential competitive intelligence exposure.\n\n1. Implement ownership validation in the basket retrieval handler. After fetching the basket from the database, compare its `UserId` field against the authenticated user's ID extracted from the JWT token. Return HTTP 403 if they do not match.\n\n2. Apply the same ownership check to all basket-related endpoints (PUT, POST for basket items) to ensure consistent access control.\n\n3. Consider using the authenticated user's ID to look up their basket rather than accepting a user-supplied basket ID:\n   ```typescript\n   const user = security.authenticatedUsers.from(req)\n   const basket = await BasketModel.findOne({ where: { UserId: user.data.id } })\n   ```\n\n4. Implement rate limiting on basket access endpoints to mitigate enumeration attacks.\n\n5. Add integration tests that verify cross-user basket access is blocked."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-601",
              "name": "Open Redirect via Allowlist Bypass in URL Redirect Endpoint",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Open Redirect via Allowlist Bypass in URL Redirect Endpoint"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An open redirect vulnerability exists in the `/redirect` endpoint due to a fundamentally flawed URL allowlist validation mechanism. The `isRedirectAllowed` function in `lib/insecurity.ts` uses `String.prototype.includes()` to check whether the user-supplied target URL contains any of the allowlisted URLs as a substring. This logic is trivially bypassable because it permits any URL that merely contains an allowed URL anywhere within it — including as a query parameter value, path component, or subdomain prefix.\n\nAn attacker can craft a redirect URL to an attacker-controlled domain that embeds an allowlisted string as a substring, causing the server to issue a 302 redirect to the malicious destination. The browser follows the redirect transparently, enabling phishing attacks that leverage the trusted domain as a stepping stone.\n\nThis finding confirms prior static-analysis finding vuln-0008."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An open redirect vulnerability exists in the `/redirect` endpoint due to a fundamentally flawed URL allowlist validation mechanism. The `isRedirectAllowed` function in `lib/insecurity.ts` uses `String.prototype.includes()` to check whether the user-supplied target URL contains any of the allowlisted URLs as a substring. This logic is trivially bypassable because it permits any URL that merely contains an allowed URL anywhere within it — including as a query parameter value, path component, or subdomain prefix.\n\nAn attacker can craft a redirect URL to an attacker-controlled domain that embeds an allowlisted string as a substring, causing the server to issue a 302 redirect to the malicious destination. The browser follows the redirect transparently, enabling phishing attacks that leverage the trusted domain as a stepping stone.\n\nThis finding confirms prior static-analysis finding vuln-0008.\n\nSuccessful exploitation enables:\n\n- Credential phishing: an attacker constructs a link on the trusted Juice Shop domain that redirects to an attacker-controlled look-alike page, harvesting user credentials\n- OAuth/session token theft: if combined with authentication flows that pass tokens in redirect URIs\n- Malware distribution: users trust the initial domain and follow the redirect to a malicious payload\n- Brand and trust erosion: the legitimate domain is used as a launchpad for attacks, damaging reputation\n\nThe vulnerability requires no authentication and can be exploited with a single crafted link sent to a victim.\n\n1. Replace the `includes()` substring check with strict URL parsing and exact origin comparison. Parse the user-supplied URL using the WHATWG URL constructor, then compare the resulting scheme and hostname against the allowlist:\n\n   ```typescript\n   export const isRedirectAllowed = (url: string) => {\n     try {\n       const parsed = new URL(url)\n       return redirectAllowlist.has(parsed.origin + parsed.pathname)\n     } catch {\n       return false\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. If path-level matching is required, compare the parsed URL's origin and pathname prefix against allowlisted entries rather than checking substring containment.\n\n3. Reject URLs with unexpected schemes (only allow `https://`).\n\n4. Consider removing the server-side redirect endpoint entirely and implementing client-side navigation with user-visible confirmation (\"You are being redirected to an external site...\").\n\n5. Add integration tests that verify bypass patterns (query embedding, path embedding, subdomain tricks) are blocked."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-943",
              "name": "NoSQL Injection in Product Reviews Update Enables Mass Data Manipulation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "NoSQL Injection in Product Reviews Update Enables Mass Data Manipulation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A NoSQL injection vulnerability exists in the product reviews update endpoint at `PATCH /rest/products/reviews`. The handler passes the user-supplied `id` field from the JSON request body directly into a MongoDB query filter without type validation or sanitization. Combined with the `{ multi: true }` option, this enables an authenticated attacker to manipulate all review documents in the collection with a single request.\n\nThe vulnerability was discovered during crystal-box DAST validation. While the prior SAST finding vuln-0006 (NoSQL injection in order tracking) is runtime-gated by Docker safety mode, this related NoSQL injection in the reviews endpoint has no such gating (DisabledEnv is None for the noSqlReviewsChallenge) and is fully exploitable against the running instance."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A NoSQL injection vulnerability exists in the product reviews update endpoint at `PATCH /rest/products/reviews`. The handler passes the user-supplied `id` field from the JSON request body directly into a MongoDB query filter without type validation or sanitization. Combined with the `{ multi: true }` option, this enables an authenticated attacker to manipulate all review documents in the collection with a single request.\n\nThe vulnerability was discovered during crystal-box DAST validation. While the prior SAST finding vuln-0006 (NoSQL injection in order tracking) is runtime-gated by Docker safety mode, this related NoSQL injection in the reviews endpoint has no such gating (DisabledEnv is None for the noSqlReviewsChallenge) and is fully exploitable against the running instance.\n\nAn authenticated attacker can:\n\n- Modify the content of all product reviews simultaneously by injecting a MongoDB operator (e.g., `{\"$ne\": \"\"}`) as the document selector, which matches every document in the collection.\n- Deface all product reviews across the entire application, destroying user-generated content integrity.\n- Forge reviews by modifying the content of reviews authored by other users, since the author field is not re-validated on update.\n- Undermine the trust and integrity of the review system, which may influence purchasing decisions.\n\nDuring testing, a single PATCH request modified 31 review documents simultaneously, replacing all review messages with attacker-controlled content.\n\n1. Validate the type of the `id` field before using it in a MongoDB query. Reject any value that is not a plain string to prevent operator injection:\n\n```typescript\nif (typeof req.body.id !== 'string') {\n  return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid review ID format' });\n}\n```\n\n2. Remove the `{ multi: true }` option from the update call. A review update should only ever modify a single document. Use `updateOne()` instead of `update()` to make the single-document intent explicit.\n\n3. Implement authorization checks to verify that the authenticated user is the author of the review being modified.\n\n4. Apply input sanitization using a library such as `mongo-sanitize` to strip MongoDB operator keys (prefixed with `$`) from user-supplied input at the middleware level."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-943"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/943.html"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "results": [
